<section class="om-reference" aria-label="Chemical cost and days of supply">
  <div class="om-split">
    <div class="om-panel-card">
      <span class="om-badge" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
      <h3>Dollars per day</h3>
      <div class="om-scenario">
        <span class="om-scenario-label">BUDGET QUESTION</span>
        <p>
          You are running alum at <strong>25 mg/L</strong> through <strong>3.0 MGD</strong>. Alum lands at <strong>$0.40/lb</strong>.
          What is coagulation costing per day?
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="om-steps">POUNDS FIRST, THEN DOLLARS</p>
      <p class="om-inline-math">25 &times; 8.34 &times; 3.0 = <b>626 lb/day</b></p>
      <p class="om-inline-math">626 &times; $0.40 = <b>$250/day</b></p>
      <p>
        About <strong>$7,500 a month</strong>. That is the number that makes a
        jar test worth running &mdash; drop the dose 2 mg/L and you have found
        real money.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="om-panel-card">
      <span class="om-badge is-blue" aria-hidden="true">&#9719;</span>
      <h3>Days of supply</h3>
      <div class="om-scenario">
        <span class="om-scenario-label">INVENTORY CHECK</span>
        <p>
          You are feeding fluoride at <strong>0.8 mg/L</strong> into <strong>2.0 MGD</strong> and there is <strong>600 lb</strong> on the
          pad. How long does that last?
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="om-steps">POUNDS FIRST, THEN DIVIDE THE SHELF BY IT</p>
      <p class="om-inline-math">0.8 &times; 8.34 &times; 2.0 = <b>13.3 lb/day</b></p>
      <p class="om-inline-math">600 &divide; 13.3 = <b>45 days</b></p>
      <p>
        Forty-five days is when you run <strong>dry</strong>, not when you
        order. Back off your lead time and a safety margin &mdash; on a two-week
        delivery, you are placing that order around day 25.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="om-note is-watch">
    <span class="om-note-label">THE MISTAKE IS STOPPING EARLY</span>
    <p>
      Both of these are a pie chart plus <strong>one more step</strong>, and the
      pounds in the middle look like a finished answer. They are not. If the
      question was dollars, keep going to dollars. If it was days, keep going to
      days.
    </p>
    <p>
      Also watch the difference between <strong>running out</strong> and <strong>reordering</strong>. If you keep a minimum on the pad, subtract it
      before you divide &mdash; 600 lb with a 150 lb floor is 450 usable pounds,
      which is 34 days, not 45.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="om-note">
    <p>
      <strong>Rising cost, steady dose?</strong> Same logic runs backwards. If
      your dollars per day climb while the dose and the price stay put, then
      flow went up &mdash; or someone changed the setpoint and did not write it
      down.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
